vered. But we have used all 4 sunny lines. So we need to cover (3,1) and (4,2) with the non-sunny line (vertical x=1) we used earlier? No, vertical x=1 covers column 1 only. So we need to cover (3,1) and (4,2) with the non-sunny line? We have only one non-sunny line left (vertical x=1). So we cannot cover them. So k=4 seems impossible for n=5.\n\nBut maybe we could use a different non-sunny line, like a horizontal line y=1, which would cover (2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1). That would cover many points. Let's try a different approach: Use a non-sunny line that is horizontal y=1. That covers (2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1). Then we have remaining points: column 1: (1,1)-(1,5) still uncovered? Actually we haven't covered column 1 yet. We could use vertical x=1 as non-sunny line, but we only have one non-sunny line (since k=4, n=5, n-k=1). So we cannot have both vertical x=1 and horizontal y=1. So we need to choose one non-sunny line. Let's try vertical x=1 (covers (1,1)-(1,5)). Then we have points in columns 2-5 as before. We need to cover them with 4 sunny lines. We attempted earlier and found difficulty. Maybe there is a clever arrangement that works. Let's try to systematically see if we can cover all points in columns 2-5 with 4 sunny lines.\n\nWe have 10 points to cover with 4 lines, each line can have at most one point per column, so each line can have at most 4 points (since there are 4 columns). So total capacity is at most 4*4 = 16 points, which is enough. But we need to assign points to lines such that each line is a straight line (with slope not 0, infinite, -1). This is a combinatorial geometry problem. For n=5, maybe it's possible. Let's try to find a construction.\n\nWe need to assign each point (x,y) with x ∈ {2,3,4,5} and y ≤ n+1 - x (so y ≤ 6 - x). So points:\n\nx=2: y=1,2,3,4\nx=3: y=1,2,3\nx=4: y=1,2\nx=5: y=1\n\nWe need to partition these 10 points into 4 lines L1,...,L4, each line not vertical/horizontal/slope -1.\n\nOne approach: Use lines of slope 2 (or any rational slope) that pass through points in different columns. Let's try to find lines that pass through 3 points each, maybe.\n\nWe can try to find a line that passes through (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) but (4,3) is not a required point. So not good.\n\nLine through (2,1),(3,3) slope = (3-1)/(3-2) = 2, passes through (4,5) not required. So covers 2 points.\n\nLine through (2,2),(3,4) but (3,4) not required (max y for x=3 is 3). So not good.\n\nLine through (2,3),(3,5) not required.\n\nLine through (2,4),(3,6) not required.\n\nSo any line through (2,*) and (3,*) will have slope (b2 - b1)/1 = integer difference. Many will be slope >0 or0. But we need to ensure that the line does not pass through any other required points that cause conflict? Actually it's okay if it passes through other required points; that would be good because it covers more points. But we need to avoid slope -1, 0, infinite.\n\nLet's try to find lines that pass through (2,4) and (4,2). Slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So can't use that.\n\nLine through (2,4) and (5,1) slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,4) and (3,1) slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, allowed (slope -3). That line also passes through (4,-2) not required. So covers (2,4),(3,1). Good.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (4,1) slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3, allowed. That line also passes through (3,5/3?) not integer.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, allowed.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (5,1) slope = (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3, allowed.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (3,2) slope = 1, allowed.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (4,2) slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2, allowed.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (5,1) slope = 0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (3,3) and (5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-3) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (3,3) and (4,2) slope = (2-3)/(4-3) = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine t